.\" fast-highlight(5): configuration, theme, and command spec files.
.TH FAST-HIGHLIGHT 5 2026-10-04 "fast-highlight 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
fast-highlight \- configuration, theme, and command spec files for fast-highlight
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB config /config.toml
.br
.IB config /theme.toml
.br
.IB config /themes/ name .toml
.br
.IB config /specs/*.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fast-highlight (1)
reads three kinds of TOML file from its config directory, written
.I config
on this page:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR config.toml ,
the size limits, log settings, and theme name;
.IP \(bu 2
a theme, which maps token types to zsh highlight styles;
.IP \(bu 2
command specs, which declare the subcommands and options of individual commands.
.PP
The config directory is
.B $FAST_HIGHLIGHT_CONFIG_DIR
when that variable is set and not empty, else
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fast-highlight
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set to an absolute path, else
.BR $HOME/.config/fast-highlight .
.B fast-highlight check-config
prints the directory and validates every file in it.
.PP
Every file is optional.
A missing
.B config.toml
yields the defaults, a missing theme yields the built-in
.B truecolor
theme, and with no spec files
only the built-in specs apply.
.PP
The daemon reads
.B config.toml
and the spec files when it starts.
The plugin reads the theme, through
.BR "fast-highlight styles" ,
when it loads.
The zsh function
.B fasthl-reload
does both again, so it applies a change to any of these files to the current shell.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.B config.toml
has one top-level key and two tables.
Every key is optional, and an unknown key is an error.
When the file has an error, the daemon uses the default configuration for every key and writes
the error to its log.
.TP
.BR theme " (string)"
The name of the theme to use, as described under
.BR "THEME SELECTION" .
A name is not empty, does not start with
.BR . ,
and does not contain
.BR / .
.SS The [limits] table
Sizes are measured in bytes of the text being highlighted, which is
.B PREBUFFER
followed by
.BR BUFFER .
.TP
.BR lex-only-bytes " (integer, default 10240)"
Above this size, the daemon skips command and filesystem lookups and reports only the syntax
of the text.
.TP
.BR hard-cap-bytes " (integer, default 65536)"
Above this size, the daemon returns no highlighting.
The value is greater than 0 and at least
.BR lex-only-bytes .
The plugin does not send a command line longer than
.B FASTHL_MAX_LENGTH
bytes, which defaults to the same 65536, so raising this limit has an effect only together with
that variable.
.TP
.BR max-spans " (integer, default 500)"
The most spans the daemon returns for one request.
A longer list is cut to its first
.B max-spans
spans.
The value is greater than 0.
.TP
.BR max-path-checks " (integer, default 64)"
The maximum number of filesystem checks per highlight request.
A cached result does not count against it.
Words beyond the limit are not checked.
.TP
.BR path-cache-ttl-ms " (integer, default 1000)"
How long a cached filesystem check result stays valid, in milliseconds.
.SS The [log] table
.TP
.BR timing " (boolean, default false)"
Writes one timing line per request to the daemon's log, as
.B serve \-\-timing
does.
.TP
.BR file " (string)"
The daemon's log file.
The value is an absolute path, or starts with
.B \(ti/
or is
.BR \(ti ,
which expand to the home directory.
.BI \(ti user
is not expanded and is an error.
A
.B \-\-log
option given to
.B fast-highlight serve
takes precedence.
When the key is absent, the daemon uses the default location given under
.B LOGGING
in
.BR fast-highlight (1).
.SH THEME SELECTION
When
.B config.toml
has no
.B theme
key, the theme is
.IB config /theme.toml
if that file exists, and the built-in
.B truecolor
theme otherwise.
.PP
When
.B theme
is set to
.IR name ,
the theme is
.IB config /themes/ name .toml
if that file exists, and the built-in theme called
.I name
otherwise.
It is an error when neither exists.
.PP
The built-in themes are:
.TP
.B default
Basic colours only, for dark and light terminals and for terminals without truecolor support.
.TP
.B truecolor
The default theme.
24-bit colours for dark terminals, inheriting from
.BR default .
It needs a terminal with truecolor support, or the
.B zsh/nearcolor
module on 88- and 256-colour terminals.
.PP
.B fast-highlight styles
prints the resolved styles of the active theme.
.SH THEME FILES
A theme file has these top-level keys, all optional.
An unknown key is an error.
.TP
.BR inherits " (string, default \(dqtruecolor\(dq)"
The theme this one is layered over: the name of a built-in theme, or
.B none
for an empty base.
A theme file cannot inherit from another theme file.
.TP
.B [meta]
Informational keys
.B name
and
.BR description ,
both strings.
They are checked for typos and otherwise ignored.
.TP
.B [styles]
One entry per token type, keyed by the token type names listed under
.BR "TOKEN TYPES" .
An entry replaces the inherited style of that token type.
An unknown token type is an error.
.PP
A style is either a string or a table.
.SS Style strings
A style string is a comma-separated list of items, in the syntax zsh 5.9 accepts in
.BR region_highlight .
Whitespace around each item is removed.
.TP
.BI fg= colour
The foreground colour.
.TP
.BI bg= colour
The background colour.
.TP
.BR bold ", " underline ", " standout
Attributes.
zsh 5.9 does not support italic or faint, and neither is accepted.
.TP
.B none
No styling.
It is valid only on its own.
.PP
A
.I colour
is one of
.BR black ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR white ,
and
.BR default ;
a number from 0 to 255; or
.BI # rgb
or
.BI # rrggbb
in hexadecimal.
Each item appears at most once in a style.
.PP
The empty string leaves the token type unstyled, removing any style it would inherit.
.SS Style tables
A style table has the keys
.B fg
and
.BR bg ,
each a colour string or an integer from 0 to 255, and
.BR bold ,
.BR underline ,
and
.BR standout ,
each a boolean.
An empty table leaves the token type unstyled.
The table
.B {\ fg\ =\ \(dqred\(dq,\ bold\ =\ true\ }
is the same style as the string
.BR \(dqfg=red,bold\(dq .
.SH TOKEN TYPES
These are the token types the daemon reports and that themes and the plugin's
.B FASTHL_STYLES
table style.
Spans nest: a string can contain a parameter, and the nested span is applied after the span that
contains it.
.TP
.B default
Plain text with no particular meaning.
It is rarely reported, since unstyled text needs no span.
.TP
.B error
A syntax error, an unknown command, an unknown subcommand, or an invalid option.
.TP
.B reserved-word
A reserved word in command position:
.BR if ,
.BR then ,
.BR for ,
.BR { ,
.BR [[ ,
.BR ! ,
and so on.
.TP
.B alias
A command word that names a regular alias.
.TP
.B suffix-alias
A command word that is resolved through a suffix alias
.RB ( "alias \-s" ).
.TP
.B global-alias
A word anywhere on the line that names a global alias
.RB ( "alias \-g" ).
.TP
.B function
A command word that names a shell function, or the name in a function definition.
.TP
.B builtin
A command word that names a shell builtin.
.TP
.B command
A command word that names an external command found in
.B PATH
or by an explicit path.
.TP
.B precommand
A precommand modifier or wrapper:
.BR sudo ,
.BR noglob ,
.BR nocorrect ,
.BR exec ,
.BR command ,
.BR builtin ,
.BR env ,
.BR time ,
and similar commands.
A function or global alias with the same name as a precommand shadows it
and gets its own type,
unless a regular alias or a reserved word of the same name also exists;
a regular alias keeps the word a precommand, so
.B "alias sudo=\(aqsudo \(aq"
keeps
.B sudo
a precommand, and the reserved words
.B time
and
.B nocorrect
outrank a function in the same way.
A quoted word skips alias lookup, so
.B \e\&sudo
is the function
.B sudo
when one is defined.
This shadowing applies to every command word except the one directly after
.B builtin
or
.BR command ,
which skip functions and aliases: the word after
.B builtin
must be a builtin, and the word after
.B command
an external command
(see
.BR "Wrapped commands and path mode" ).
.TP
.B separator
A command separator or pipeline operator:
.BR ; ,
.BR & ,
.BR && ,
.BR || ,
.BR | ,
.BR |& ,
.BR &! ,
and
.BR &| .
.TP
.B redirection
A redirection operator with any file descriptor prefix:
.BR > ,
.BR 2> ,
.BR >&2 ,
.BR << ,
.BR <<< ,
.BR &> ,
and so on.
.TP
.B heredoc
A here-document delimiter word and the here-document body.
.TP
.B single-quoted
A single-quoted string,
.BR \(aq...\(aq .
.TP
.B double-quoted
A double-quoted string,
.BR \(dq...\(dq .
.TP
.B dollar-quoted
A dollar-quoted string,
.BR $\(aq...\(aq .
.TP
.B backquoted
A backquoted command substitution,
.BR \(ga...\(ga ,
delimiters included.
.TP
.B escape
A backslash escape, inside or outside quotes.
.TP
.B parameter
A parameter expansion:
.BR $name ,
.BR ${...} ,
.BR $1 ,
.BR $? ,
and so on.
.TP
.B substitution
The delimiters of a command substitution,
.B $(
and
.BR ) .
.TP
.B process-substitution
The delimiters of a process substitution,
.BR <( ,
.BR >( ,
or
.BR =( ,
and
.BR ) .
.TP
.B arithmetic
An arithmetic expansion or command,
.B $((...))\&
or
.BR ((...)) .
.TP
.B glob
Glob characters and extended glob operators:
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
.BR [...] ,
.BR ** ,
.BR # ,
.BR ^ ,
and
.BR \(ti .
.TP
.B glob-qualifier
A glob qualifier list at the end of a word, such as
.B (.)\&
or
.BR (om[1,3]) .
.TP
.B brace-expansion
A brace expansion,
.B {a,b}
or
.BR {1..10} .
.TP
.B history-expansion
A history expansion:
.BR !! ,
.BR !$ ,
.BR !\-2 ,
.BR ^old^new ,
and so on.
.TP
.B comment
A comment.
Comments are recognised only when
.B INTERACTIVE_COMMENTS
is set.
.TP
.B assignment
The
.B name=
or
.B name+=
part of an assignment, and the parentheses of an array value.
.TP
.B grouping
Subshell and group delimiters
.B (
and
.BR ) ,
and the parentheses of a function definition.
.TP
.B operator
An operator inside
.BR "[[ ... ]]" :
.BR \-f ,
.BR == ,
.BR =\(ti ,
.BR && ,
.BR ! ,
.BR < ,
and so on.
.TP
.B path
An argument that names an existing file.
.TP
.B path-directory
An argument that names an existing directory.
.TP
.B path-prefix
An argument that is a prefix of an existing path, while it is still being typed.
.TP
.B subcommand
A subcommand known to the command's spec, such as
.B commit
in
.BR "git commit" .
.TP
.B option
An option known to the command's spec, such as
.B \-\-verbose
or
.BR \-C .
.SH COMMAND SPEC FILES
A command spec declares the subcommands and options of one command, so that the daemon reports
known ones as
.B subcommand
and
.B option
and, where the spec says its lists are complete, unknown ones as
.BR error .
Each spec is one TOML file.
The file name is irrelevant; the
.B name
key identifies the command.
.PP
The built-in specs are compiled into the binary.
They cover the commands with subcommands
.BR git ,
.BR cargo ,
.BR docker ,
.BR kubectl ,
.BR systemctl ,
.BR npm ,
.BR podman ,
.BR gh ,
.BR apt ,
.BR dnf ,
.BR brew ,
.BR pip ,
.BR uv ,
.BR go ,
.BR rustup ,
and
.BR ip ;
the commands with options only
.BR make ,
.BR ssh ,
.BR tar ,
.BR journalctl ,
and
.BR rm ;
and the precommands
.BR sudo ,
.BR doas ,
.BR env ,
.BR nice ,
.BR nohup ,
.BR time ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR stdbuf ,
.BR ionice ,
.BR chrt ,
.BR taskset ,
.BR xargs ,
.BR noglob ,
.BR nocorrect ,
.BR exec ,
.BR command ,
.BR builtin ,
and
.BR \- .
The regular files whose names end in
.B .toml
in
.IB config /specs
are read after the built-ins, in file-name order.
.SS Top-level keys
.TP
.BR name " (string, required)"
The command name.
.TP
.BR aliases " (array of strings, default [])"
Other command names that share this spec.
.TP
.BR merge " (boolean, default false)"
In a user file: merge into the existing spec of the same name instead of replacing it.
.TP
.BR common-options " (array of strings, default [])"
Options valid at the top level and at every subcommand level, at any depth.
.TP
.BR precommand " (boolean, default false)"
The command runs another command, as
.BR sudo ,
.BR env ,
and
.B nice
do.
The top level wraps with
.B positional = 0
unless it sets
.B positional
or
.BR wrapped-after .
.TP
.BR skip-assignments " (boolean, default false)"
When the top level wraps:
.IB NAME = value
words before the wrapped command are skipped, as for
.BR env ,
also when the value contains an expansion.
.PP
The top level also accepts every level key.
.SS Level keys
These keys are valid at the top level and in every
.BI [subcommands. name ]
table.
Subcommand tables nest to any depth, as in
.BR [subcommands.remote.subcommands.add] .
.TP
.BR options " (array of strings, default [])"
Options valid at this level only.
.TP
.BR subcommands " (table, default {})"
The subcommands of this level, keyed by name.
.TP
.BR aliases " (array of strings, default [])"
Subcommand tables only: other names for this subcommand, as
.B ps
is for
.BR "docker container ls" .
.TP
.BR complete " (boolean, default false)"
The subcommand list is complete: an unknown word in subcommand position is an error.
.TP
.BR options-complete " (boolean, default false)"
The option list is complete: an unknown option is an error.
.TP
.BR options-first " (boolean, default false)"
Options are recognised only before the first plain argument, and later words are plain, as in
.BI "docker run " "image command " \-x\fR.
.TP
.BR abbreviations " (boolean, default false)"
A unique prefix of a subcommand name or alias selects that subcommand, as
.B inst
does in
.BR "npm inst" .
For options, see
.BR option-abbreviations .
.TP
.BR option-abbreviations " (boolean, default false)"
A unique prefix of a declared long option selects that option, as
.B \-\-verb
selects
.BR \-\-verbose ,
in the way GNU
.BR getopt_long (3)
accepts abbreviations.
See
.B Long-option abbreviations
below.
For subcommands, see
.BR abbreviations .
.TP
.BR positional " (integer, unset by default)"
The level wraps a command: the number of plain arguments between the options and the wrapped
command, as in
.BI timeout " duration command" .
.TP
.BR wrapped-after " (string, unset by default)"
The level wraps the command after the first
.BR \-\- ,
as in
.BI "kubectl exec " pod " \-\- " command\fR.
.B \(dq\-\-\(dq
is the only accepted value.
.TP
.BR path-mode-options " (array of strings, default [])"
Options of this level that make the remaining words files rather than a command, as
.B \-e
does in
.BI "sudo \-e " file\fR.
.PP
A level sets at most one of
.B positional
and
.BR wrapped-after .
See
.B Wrapped commands and path mode
below.
.PP
A level inherits nothing from its parent except
.B common-options
and
.BR option-abbreviations .
A level that leaves
.B option-abbreviations
unset uses its parent's setting, and an explicit value applies to that level and to its
descendants that leave it unset.
Options declared in a level's
.B options
apply only at that level, so the built-in
.B git
spec recognises
.B \-\-git\-dir=
before a subcommand and treats it as unknown after
.BR commit ,
whose level does not declare it.
.SS Option syntax
Each entry in
.BR options ,
.BR common-options ,
and
.B path-mode-options
is one spelling of one option.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose ", " \-name
A flag that takes no value.
.TP
.BR \-m= ", " \-\-message=
An option with a required value.
The value is accepted attached
.RB ( \-\-message=text ,
and for single-letter options
.BR \-mtext )
or as the next word
.RB ( "\-\-message text" ,
.BR "\-m text" ).
The value word is a plain argument.
.TP
.BR \-S=? ", " \-\-color=?
An option with an optional value, accepted only attached
.RB ( \-\-color=always ,
.BR \-Skey ).
The next word is never consumed.
.TP
.BR +* ", " \-\-no\-*
A pattern.
A trailing
.B *
matches every word that starts with the text before it.
A pattern takes no separate value.
A pattern is the only way a word that does not start with
.B \-
is an option, as
.B +nightly
is in
.BR "cargo +nightly build" .
.B path-mode-options
takes no patterns.
.PP
Every other entry starts with
.BR \- ;
an entry that does not is an invalid option.
Single-letter options bundle:
.B \-av
is
.B \-a \-v
when every letter is a declared single-letter option.
A letter that takes a value ends the bundle, and the rest of the word, or else the next word, is
its value, as in
.BR "\-xzf archive.tar" .
.SS Long-option abbreviations
With
.BR "option-abbreviations = true" ,
a word that starts with
.B \-\-
and is not a declared spelling is matched by its stem, the text before the first
.BR = .
The stem selects an option when it is a proper prefix of exactly one declared spelling that
starts with
.BR \-\- ,
and the word then takes that option's arity: for
.BR \-\-output= ,
.B \-\-out file
consumes
.B file
and
.B \-\-out=file
is complete; for the flag
.BR \-\-verbose ,
.B \-\-verb=1
is unknown.
.IP \(bu 2
Only long options abbreviate.
A word with a single
.B \-
never does.
.IP \(bu 2
Only declared spellings count, and patterns are never candidates.
A spec that enables the key should declare every long option of its command; otherwise a prefix
that the command finds ambiguous selects the one option that is declared.
.IP \(bu 2
An exact spelling takes precedence over a prefix:
.B \-\-exclude
is
.B \-\-exclude
even when
.B \-\-exclude\-caches
is declared, and
.B \-\-flag=x
for a declared flag
.B \-\-flag
is unknown.
.IP \(bu 2
The rule is strict: a stem that matches more than one spelling is ambiguous, even when the
spellings are aliases of one option with the same arity, as
.B \-\-colo
is for
.B \-\-color
and
.BR \-\-colour ;
.BR getopt_long (3)
would accept it.
.IP \(bu 2
An ambiguous stem and a stem that matches nothing are unknown options, which are errors only
where the level has
.BR "options-complete = true" .
.PP
The built-in specs that enable it are
.BR tar ,
.BR make ,
.BR xargs ,
.BR rm ,
.BR journalctl ,
.BR env ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR nice ,
.BR nohup ,
.BR stdbuf ,
.BR time ,
.BR ionice ,
.BR taskset ,
and
.BR chrt .
.SS Classification
The daemon walks a command's arguments in order, starting at the top level of its spec.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-
is an option and ends option parsing; every later word is plain.
At an
.B options-first
level, every word after the first plain argument is plain,
.B \-\-
included.
.IP \(bu 2
A word that is just
.B \-
is plain, unless
.B \-
is declared as an option.
.IP \(bu 2
A word that starts with
.BR \- ,
or matches a pattern, is a known option or an unknown option.
An unknown option is an error only at a level with
.BR "options-complete = true" .
A word of the form
.BI \- digits
is never an error.
With
.BR option-abbreviations ,
a unique prefix of a long option is that option, and an ambiguous or unmatched prefix is an
unknown option.
.IP \(bu 2
The first plain word at a level is in subcommand position.
A known subcommand name or alias there is reported as
.BR subcommand ,
and classification continues at that subcommand's level.
An unknown word there is an error only at a level that has subcommands and
.BR "complete = true" .
A word that contains an expansion is never an error.
.IP \(bu 2
After the first plain word at a level, no further subcommand matching happens at that level.
.SS Wrapped commands and path mode
A level wraps a command when it sets
.B positional
or
.BR wrapped-after .
The top level of a spec with
.B precommand = true
wraps with
.B positional = 0
unless it sets one of them.
A top level that wraps without
.B precommand
gets no
.B precommand
span.
Before
.B positional
became a level key, a top-level
.B positional
without
.B precommand
was ignored; it now wraps.
.PP
The daemon descends through subcommands as in classification, and only the level it ends at
counts; when that level does not wrap, there is no wrapped command.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BI "positional = " n\fR,
the daemon skips the level's options and their values, with unknown options, ambiguous
abbreviations included, assumed to be flags; then
.IB NAME = value
words when
.B skip-assignments = true
and the level is the top level; then
.I n
plain words.
The next word is the wrapped command.
Option parsing stops at
.B \-\-
or at the first word that is not an option, as POSIX
.BR getopt (3)
does.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR "wrapped-after = \(dq\-\-\(dq" ,
options are parsed as in classification, and the word after the first
.B \-\-
is the wrapped command, after
.IB NAME = value
words when
.B skip-assignments = true
and the level is the top level.
With no
.BR \-\- ,
or nothing after it, there is no wrapped command.
At an
.B options-first
level,
.B \-\-
is found only in option position; after a plain word it is plain.
.IP \(bu 2
The wrapped command is classified as a command word in its own right, against the local command
table, so a command that exists only in a container or pod is reported as
.BR error .
The arguments of a precommand before it are classified by its spec and not checked as paths; the
arguments of another command before it are highlighted as usual.
.PP
The word after
.B builtin
or
.B command
is looked up as zsh does:
.IP \(bu 2
After
.BR builtin ,
only a builtin is accepted.
A builtin that is a precommand
.RB ( builtin ,
.BR command ,
.BR exec ,
.BR noglob ,
.BR \- )
is a
.B precommand
and its chain continues.
Any other word is an
.BR error :
an external command, a function, an alias, a reserved word such as
.BR time ,
a path, an unknown name, and
.B \-\-
.RB ( builtin
takes no options).
.IP \(bu 2
After
.BR command ,
only an external command is accepted: a command found in
.BR PATH ,
or a path to an executable file.
Functions, aliases, builtins, reserved words, and precommand builtins
.RB ( "command builtin echo" ,
.BR "command noglob ls" )
are errors.
An external precommand is a
.B precommand
and its chain continues, even when a function of the same name exists:
.B command time ls
and
.B command nice ls
run the programs.
A
.B PATH
binary named
.B command
or
.B builtin
makes
.B command command
a precommand in the same way.
.IP \(bu 2
.B command
takes the options
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-v ,
and
.BR \-V ,
combined in one word in any order
.RB ( \-pv ,
.BR \-Vp ),
and
.B \-\-
ends them.
Several option words
.RB ( "\-p \-v ls" )
are accepted only when one of them has a
.B v
or
.BR V ;
otherwise one option word is the most, and in
.B command \-p \-p ls
the first
.B \-p
is the command word, an error.
Any other word, an unknown option
.RB ( "command \-x ls" )
or a lone
.B \-
included, is the command word; in
.BR "command \-\- \-v ls" ,
.B \-v
is the command word.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.B \-v
or
.BR \-V ,
.B command
runs nothing: every remaining word is a lookup.
Each gets the type of what it names (alias, global or suffix alias, function, builtin, reserved
word, or command), whether quoted or not, and is an
.B error
only when it names nothing, so
.B command \-v myfunc
is not an error.
A lookup never continues a precommand chain.
.IP \(bu 2
A word that
.B builtin
or
.B command
rejects wraps nothing: its arguments are only checked as paths, so
.B builtin git status
does not classify
.B status
as a
.B git
subcommand.
A word with an expansion or a glob is never an error.
A quoted word is looked up by its value, and no alias is expanded.
With
.BR AUTO_CD ,
a directory after either word is not a command.
While the word is being typed, a prefix of a name the lookup accepts (a builtin after
.BR builtin ,
a
.B PATH
command after
.BR command ,
any name after
.BR "command \-v" )
is not highlighted.
.PP
The specs of
.B builtin
and
.B command
decide only whether they are precommands; the lookups and options above are built in and ignore
spec overrides.
Global aliases after these words, the default
.B PATH
search of
.BR "command \-p" ,
and the
.B POSIX_BUILTINS
option are not modelled.
.PP
A word is
.IB NAME = value
when, after quote removal, it starts with an ASCII shell identifier and
.B =
before any expansion:
.BR FOO=1 ,
.BR FOO=$x ,
and
.B \(dqFOO\(dq=$(date)
are skipped;
.B FOO$x=1
and
.B $cmd
are not.
.PP
The built-in
.B kubectl
spec sets
.B wrapped-after = \(dq\-\-\(dq
for
.BR exec ,
so
.B kubectl exec mypod \-\- ls \-l
reports
.B ls
as a command and classifies
.B \-l
by the spec of
.BR ls ,
if it has one, while
.B kubectl exec mypod ls
has no wrapped command:
.PP
.RS
.EX
[subcommands.exec]
wrapped\-after = "\-\-"
options = ["\-c=", "\-\-container=", "\-i", "\-t"]
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B path-mode-options
lists options that make the remaining words operands, as
.B \-e
does for
.BR sudo .
Each entry declares the option at its level with the syntax of
.BR options ;
an entry also in
.B options
or
.B common-options
takes the arity given here.
This lets a user file make an existing option select path mode by listing it again;
the built-in specs do not repeat a spelling within a level.
When such an option is given at the level the descent ends at, while options are still parsed,
there is no wrapped command:
.IP \(bu 2
The option counts when the word resolves to it: an exact spelling, the name of
.BI \-\- name = value\fR,
an abbreviation, as
.B \-\-ed
is for
.B \-\-edit
with
.BR option-abbreviations ,
or a letter of a bundle up to and including the first letter that takes a value.
.B \-Ee
and
.B \-eu root
select the mode;
.B \-ue
does not, because
.B e
is the value of
.BR \-u .
Unknown and ambiguous options never select it.
.IP \(bu 2
The mode is sticky: later options are still parsed
.RB ( "\-u root"
consumes
.BR root ),
and they never cancel it.
.IB NAME = value
words are not skipped and no plain words are counted.
.IP \(bu 2
Every argument is classified and checked as a path as for a command that wraps nothing: an
existing file is reported as a path, a missing one gets no span, and words after
.B \-\-
are operands.
Option values are checked too:
.B sudo \-e \-u root f
checks
.BR root .
.IP \(bu 2
On a level that does not wrap,
.B path-mode-options
declares its options and has no other effect.
.PP
The built-in
.B sudo
spec, in outline, with which
.B sudo \-e /etc/hosts
reports
.B /etc/hosts
as a path and
.B sudo ls
still reports
.B ls
as a command:
.PP
.RS
.EX
name = "sudo"
precommand = true
options\-first = true
path\-mode\-options = ["\-e", "\-\-edit"]
options = ["\-u=", "\-\-user=", "\-E"]
.EE
.RE
.SS Loading and overriding
A user file whose
.B name
matches an existing spec replaces that spec entirely.
With
.B merge = true
it is merged instead: option lists and aliases are appended, with a later spelling of the same
option taking precedence; keys present in the user file override; and subcommand tables are
merged recursively by name.
Merging cannot remove anything; replacing the spec can.
A file with
.B merge = true
and no existing spec of that name is loaded as a new spec.
.B path-mode-options
is appended like
.BR options .
.B positional
and
.B wrapped-after
are one setting: a merged level that sets either replaces the level's start and clears the other.
.PP
A file that fails to parse, has an unknown key, or declares an invalid option is skipped with a
warning, and the other files still load.
So is a file with a level that sets both
.B positional
and
.BR wrapped-after ,
sets
.B wrapped-after
to anything but
.BR \(dq\-\-\(dq ,
or lists a pattern in
.BR path-mode-options ;
the warning names the command path of the level.
A user file is checked on its own before it is merged, so a file with
.B merge = true
must pass these checks by itself.
The daemon writes the warning to its log;
.B fast-highlight check-config
and
.B fast-highlight highlight
print it on standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB config /config.toml
The configuration file.
.TP
.IB config /theme.toml
The theme when
.B config.toml
has no
.B theme
key.
.TP
.IB config /themes/ name .toml
The theme selected by
.B theme = \(dq\fIname\fP\(dq
in
.BR config.toml .
.TP
.IB config /specs/*.toml
User command specs.
.SH EXAMPLES
A
.B config.toml
that selects a user theme, raises the lex-only threshold, and logs request timing:
.PP
.RS
.EX
theme = "mine"
\&
[limits]
lex\-only\-bytes = 20480
hard\-cap\-bytes = 65536
\&
[log]
timing = true
file = "\(ti/.cache/fast\-highlight.log"
.EE
.RE
.PP
The theme
.BR themes/mine.toml ,
which keeps the basic-colour
.B default
theme but greys comments, restyles errors, and leaves path prefixes
unstyled:
.PP
.RS
.EX
inherits = "default"
\&
[meta]
name = "mine"
description = "Basic colours with grey comments"
\&
[styles]
comment = { fg = 244 }
error = "fg=white,bg=red,bold"
path\-prefix = ""
.EE
.RE
.PP
A spec for a command with no built-in spec, in
.BR specs/just.toml :
.PP
.RS
.EX
name = "just"
options = ["\-f=", "\-\-justfile=", "\-l", "\-\-list", "\-n", "\-\-dry\-run", "\-\-set="]
.EE
.RE
.PP
A spec that adds a subcommand to the built-in
.B git
spec without replacing it, in
.BR specs/git\-local.toml :
.PP
.RS
.EX
name = "git"
merge = true
\&
[subcommands.sync]
options = ["\-\-dry\-run"]
.EE
.RE
.PP
A spec with nested subcommands and value-taking options.
Without
.BR "merge = true" ,
a file in this form replaces the built-in
.B git
spec:
.PP
.RS
.EX
name = "git"
common\-options = ["\-h", "\-\-help"]
options = ["\-C=", "\-c=", "\-p", "\-\-paginate", "\-\-git\-dir=", "\-\-version"]
\&
[subcommands.commit]
options = ["\-m=", "\-\-message=", "\-a", "\-\-all", "\-\-amend", "\-S=?", "\-\-gpg\-sign=?"]
\&
[subcommands.remote]
complete = true
\&
[subcommands.remote.subcommands.remove]
aliases = ["rm"]
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fast-highlight (1),
.BR zshzle (1)
.PP
The source repository is
.UR https://github.com/wolpert/fast-highlight
.UE .
